\title{Composition, Not Position:\\ Exact Accounting for a Left-Edge Excess in a\\ Quadratic-Residue Search Window}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A large stratified search records, for each modulus $N$, the residues $v = j^2 - N$ at positions $j$ ranging over the window $W(N) = [\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor + 1,\ 3\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor]$. Pooling $9594$ hits over $128$ moduli of size below $2^{96}$, the leading tenth of the window (``first decile'', $D_1$) carries an excess of $604.76$ hits against a flat, size-blind null: a pooled rate ratio of $1.637$, with a two-component mixture fit reporting $\Delta\AICc = 49.78$ against a pre-registered decision bar of $6$. The natural interpretation is a \emph{positional kernel}. We prove that no positional component is required, and that none can be inferred from the reported statistics. Four independent results account for the profile in full. (i) \emph{Inclusion geometry}: for every first-decile position, $25 v \le 11 \lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor^2 \le 11N$, exact, sharp and scale-carrying; for $N < 2^{96}$ this forces $v < 2^{95}$, so the observed count of zero first-decile hits with $\bitlen(v) \ge 96$ is a theorem rather than an observation. (ii) \emph{Exact degeneracy and a quantile identity}: at fixed $N$ the residue is a strictly increasing, explicitly invertible function of the position, so positional and magnitude weightings are observationally indistinguishable; the empirical quantile function is $\#\{j \in W(N): v \le x\} = \min(3\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor, \lfloor\sqrt{N+x}\rfloor) - \lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, and the rescaled residue obeys the limit law $F(y) = (\sqrt{1+y}-1)/2$ on $[0,8]$ with Kolmogorov error at most $1/(2M)$, attaining the value $1/10$ exactly at the decile level $y = 11/25$. (iii) \emph{Composition accounting}: the flat-referenced excess splits exactly as $\FlatExcess = \BandExcess + \Comp$; the pooled rate ratio factorises as (matched ratio) $\times$ (composition factor), and $1.097 \times 1.4924 = 1.637$ to four decimals; extremally, the composition factor lies in $[p_{\min}/p_0, p_{\max}/p_0]$ for any allocation, and at least $77\%$ of the observed excess is provably composition. (iv) \emph{Pooled versus stratified evidence}: pooled $\Delta\AICc$ is bounded by the sum of stratum values plus a null misspecification gap $G \ge 0$ plus a penalty defect; strata at $5.94$ and $-0.40$ with defect at most $3$ force $G \ge 41.2$, and the residual is exactly what a monotone size density produces at a truncation boundary, with relative edge excess $(1-r^m)/(1+r^m) \le m(1-r)$. We also calibrate the control arm: a maximal $|z|$ of $2.53$ over $128$ strata gives a multiplicity-corrected bound exceeding $1$, so ``controls clean'' means the absence of an exceedance, not confirmation of the null.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} stratified sampling; Simpson's paradox; composition bias; quadratic residues; integer square root; quantile identity; model selection; AICc; truncation bias; multiplicity correction.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The design}

Fix a modulus $N \in \mathbb{N}$ and write $s = s(N) = \lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ for its integer square root. A sieve-type search enumerates positions
\[
  j \in W(N) := [\,s+1,\ 3s\,] \cap \mathbb{Z},
\]
and at each position records the \emph{residue} $v(N,j) := j^2 - N \in \mathbb{N}$. A subset of the positions register as \emph{hits}, according to an arithmetic criterion whose details are irrelevant here. The design pools hits across $128$ moduli, all below $2^{96}$, yielding $9594$ pooled hits against $512{,}000$ control runs.

The window has $2s$ positions. Its \emph{first decile} is the leading tenth,
\[
  D_1(N) := \{\, j \in W(N) : 10(j-s) \le 2s \,\} = \{\, j \in W(N) : 5j \le 6s \,\}.
\]

\subsection{The observation, and the tempting reading}

Against a flat null assigning probability $1/10$ to $D_1$, the pooled data show an excess of $+604.76$ hits, a pooled rate ratio of $1.637$. Fitting a two-component mixture --- a background plus a component supported near the left edge --- to the \emph{kept} sample (residues truncated to $v \ge 2^{95}$) returns an edge weight $0.0403$ with interval $[0.0301, 0.0525]$ and $\Delta\AICc = 49.78$ against a pre-registered bar of $6$. The reading this invites is a \emph{positional kernel}: a component of the hit intensity depending on $j-s$ over and above any dependence on the magnitude $v$.

\subsection{The resolution}

We prove the profile is accounted for entirely by magnitude together with the geometry of the window. The argument has four independent legs (Sections \ref{sec:geom}--\ref{sec:evidence}), assembled in Section \ref{sec:verdict}; Section \ref{sec:controls} calibrates the control arm.

\subsection{Notation}

$\bitlen(v)$ is the number of binary digits of $v$, so for $v \ge 1$, $\bitlen(v) < b \iff v < 2^{b-1}$. A \emph{band} is a stratum of hits with a common range of $\bitlen(v)$; band $i$ has exposure $n_i \ge 0$, observed first-decile count $k_i \ge 0$, and band rate $p_i \ge 0$. The flat null uses a single rate $p_0 > 0$. All sums over bands are finite.

